#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a88625d-af27-53c4-a457-58e65edd2cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293696} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5399f1-9fff-5cb2-8ad0-4cb50b507b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293697} {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} {:answer/id #uuid "5a252763-0942-541b-b522-67772f90d79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293699} {:answer/id #uuid "e74db172-d46a-5ed3-82af-e90331457394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc355f65-3bf9-5528-8a74-4acdf8839e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7ee43f3-f778-5c3e-9099-6bca7ddcc6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114271" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f408129-e07b-5307-91be-0187e2e6b467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546} {:answer/id #uuid "d059a5aa-c6bc-57a4-b878-a8b01077a22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387547} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4eb391-5e68-5ef3-b269-3ba59e26f3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387548} {:answer/id #uuid "6814e970-27da-504d-9127-9a53b7cec027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387549} {:answer/id #uuid "9673ba70-1547-5334-a20e-30783bba22af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9673ba70-1547-5334-a20e-30783bba22af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387550} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ebbd00-a8f1-5c5d-aee6-4c58a601bce8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6aac796-5f94-58f0-b828-88b8f756ca51" :question/math-academy-id "q-22826" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d50be51-265e-5b4d-8db4-b7fe0b52fe02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d50be51-265e-5b4d-8db4-b7fe0b52fe02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387553} :answer-field/id #uuid "87138f5c-d35f-53d1-b15d-bbe546195b5d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e23055-b865-5582-b9cc-0d1fef853862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28e23055-b865-5582-b9cc-0d1fef853862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387555} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd9e4908-a3d0-5df9-add8-e4d5361002ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221275d8-92f3-5e6d-9da0-728827506990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221275d8-92f3-5e6d-9da0-728827506990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27c1597-d7c5-542c-a577-af710d9af063" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46b0f9ab-90c3-573d-be61-4c2031542d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083206/assets/d8fcc410e057fcfee15df2e182cdc1cf593c1b1fcf09029366e920fe48f17409.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-6,6].$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down are\n\n$(-6,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\,$\nand \n$\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-6,-4)$ and $(1,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals \n\n$$\n(-6,-4\\,)\\,\\text{and}\\,(\\,1,5\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} {:answer/id #uuid "ed071224-8daa-5dbc-b675-93c3bf27608c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356495} {:answer/id #uuid "1010bb3a-a8eb-51ee-bef0-4e4d8ebd7dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} :answer-field/id #uuid "6646c1ae-dd3e-59d7-8786-2633e5de810b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdef858-6874-5c3e-9c38-59e8a66a0ab3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/typ